// 21 · Nutrient Timing Basics: before, during, after, in plain language.
// Composition: hook = one big question, nothing else; takeaway = line top-left, question
// flush right in the lower half; alignment = left. Insight is visual-dominant (day line).

module.exports = {
  num: 21,
  slug: "nutrient-timing-basics",
  title: "Nutrient Timing Basics",
  category: "Nutrition & Fueling",
  palette: "sand",
  hero: { layout: "day-line-windows", concept: "One training day as a line from 9 a.m. to 9 p.m.: a meal window 1-4 h before practice, the session itself, and a protein window in the 2 h after" },
  compose: { hook: "question-only", takeaway: "question-right", align: "left" },
  oneIdea: "Eat enough across the whole day first; timing earns its keep when sessions run long or come close together.",
  question: "Where in our schedule do athletes train on empty?",
  caveats: `The ranges here are general guidance from position stands, not individual prescriptions; the sports dietitian sets each athlete's plan (the joint position stand itself says athletes should be referred to a registered dietitian). The "30-minute anabolic window" is not a fraud, it is oversold: protein after training helps, but the evidence points to total daily intake and regular protein feedings as the bigger levers (Schoenfeld et al. 2013; Kerksick et al. 2017). The meta-analysis is about muscle gain in resistance training, not every sport outcome. Jagim et al. 2019 is one Division II lacrosse team (n = 20, self-reported intake), so present it as an example, never as a rate. The "early session" advice on slide 4 is practical guidance consistent with the position stand's note to avoid high-fat, high-fiber choices before exercise, not a tested protocol. The day on slide 3 is illustrative.`,
  sources: [
