    { id: "kerksick2017", cite: "Kerksick CM, Arent S, Schoenfeld BJ, et al. International Society of Sports Nutrition position stand: nutrient timing. J Int Soc Sports Nutr. 2017;14:33. doi:10.1186/s12970-017-0189-4", grade: "position stand", found: "external", url: "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28919842/", supports: "Meeting total daily protein, in feedings about every 3 h, is the primary emphasis; post-exercise protein (immediately to 2 h) stimulates muscle protein synthesis; ~30-60 g/h carbohydrate in sessions over 60-70 min; carbohydrate plus protein for rapid refueling." },
    { id: "schoenfeld2013", cite: "Schoenfeld BJ, Aragon AA, Krieger JW. The effect of protein timing on muscle strength and hypertrophy: a meta-analysis. J Int Soc Sports Nutr. 2013;10(1):53. doi:10.1186/1550-2783-10-53", grade: "meta-analysis (resistance-training RCTs)", found: "external", url: "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24299050/", supports: "After controlling for covariates, protein timing showed no significant effect on strength or hypertrophy; total protein intake was the strongest predictor of hypertrophy." },
    { id: "aragon2013", cite: "Aragon AA, Schoenfeld BJ. Nutrient timing revisited: is there a post-exercise anabolic window? J Int Soc Sports Nutr. 2013;10(1):5. doi:10.1186/1550-2783-10-5", grade: "narrative review", found: "external", url: "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23360586/", supports: "The importance, even the existence, of a post-exercise window varies; pre- and post-exercise meals should not be separated by more than about 3-4 hours." },
    { id: "jagim2019", cite: "Jagim AR, Zabriskie H, Currier B, Harty PS, Stecker R, Kerksick CM. Nutrient status and perceptions of energy and macronutrient intake in a group of collegiate female lacrosse athletes. J Int Soc Sports Nutr. 2019;16(1):43. doi:10.1186/s12970-019-0314-7", grade: "single cross-sectional study (n = 20, NCAA Division II, self-reported intake)", found: "Zotero", supports: "Athletes ate significantly less than recommended values for energy, carbohydrates and protein (abstract, p. 1)." },
    { id: "kerksick2008", cite: "Kerksick C, Harvey T, Stout J, et al. International Society of Sports Nutrition position stand: nutrient timing. J Int Soc Sports Nutr. 2008;5:17. doi:10.1186/1550-2783-5-17", grade: "position stand (superseded by the 2017 update)", found: "Zotero", supports: "Planned nutrient timing around high-volume training supports recovery and repair compared with unplanned intake (p. 4)." },
  ],
  slides: [
    {
      role: "hook", time: 45,
      notes: `Quick question to start. Think about the earliest session on your calendar this week, the 6 a.m. lift or the early practice. What did your athletes eat before it?

For a lot of them, the honest answer is nothing since dinner the night before. Not because they don't care, but because the alarm went off at 5:30 and nobody planned it.

That is what nutrient timing really is. Not a magic shake at a magic minute, just making sure the fuel shows up when the work does.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline(["What did your athletes eat ", { t: "before the 6 a.m. lift?", color: "accent" }], { y: 1.75, w: 10.4, h: 3.0, size: 84 });
      },
    },
    {
      role: "problem", time: 60,
      notes: `Here is the problem. The idea that spread was the 30-minute "anabolic window": miss the shake right after the lift and the work is wasted.

The research is calmer than that. When a meta-analysis pulled the protein-timing trials together and controlled for how much protein people ate overall, timing on its own added little. Total daily protein was the strongest predictor of muscle gain. The ISSN position stand now says the same thing: hit the daily total, spread across the day.

And the daily total is often where athletes fall short. In one college lacrosse team that logged its food, athletes ate less than recommended for energy, carbohydrate and protein. So before we fuss over minutes, we need to make sure the day is fed.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("The 30-minute window got the attention. The day matters more.", { y: 1.2, w: 11.5, h: 1.6, size: 48 });
        s.text(0.75, 3.15, 8.0, 1.9, "Total daily protein predicted muscle gain best. Timing alone added little.", { font: "light", size: 28, lineH: 1.15 });
        s.text(0.75, 4.75, 8.0, 1.2, "And one college team ate less than recommended for energy, carbs and protein.", { font: "light", size: 22, color: "muted", lineH: 1.2 });
        s.source("Schoenfeld, Aragon & Krieger 2013 · Kerksick et al. 2017 · Jagim et al. 2019", { y: 6.2, refs: ["schoenfeld2013", "kerksick2017", "jagim2019"] });
        s.cite("aragon2013");
      },
    },
    {
      role: "insight", time: 75,
      notes: `So when does timing matter? Picture one training day as a line, with practice in the afternoon.

Before: a real meal one to four hours out, so the athlete starts with fuel in the tank. During: if the session runs past about an hour, carbohydrate helps, from a sports drink or a snack at breaks. After: protein within a couple of hours, as part of a regular rhythm through the day.

Where timing really earns its keep is when sessions are long, or close together. Two sessions less than eight hours apart, a doubleheader, a tournament day: then what you eat right after the first one decides how you start the second. Outside those cases, a well-fed day does most of the work.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("Timing matters most when sessions run long or close together.", { y: 1.2, w: 10.5, h: 1.25, size: 40 });
        const X = h => 0.9 + (h - 9) * 0.95; // 9 a.m. to 9 p.m.
        const base = 5.15, top = 2.95;
        // The two fueling windows (quiet fill) and the session itself (accent outline).
        s.rect(X(11), top, X(14) - X(11), base - top, { fill: "soft" });
        s.rect(X(17), top, X(19) - X(17), base - top, { fill: "soft" });
        s.rect(X(15), top, X(17) - X(15), base - top, { line: "accent", lineW: 2.5 });
        s.line(X(9), base, X(21), base, { color: "muted", w: 1.5 });
        [[9, "9 AM"], [12, "Noon"], [15, "3 PM"], [18, "6 PM"], [21, "9 PM"]].forEach(([h, t]) => {
          s.line(X(h), base, X(h), base + 0.12, { color: "muted", w: 1 });
          s.label(t, { x: X(h) - 0.8, y: base + 0.2, w: 1.6, align: "center", size: 14 });
        });
        const win = [
          [11, 14, "Before", "A meal 1–4 h out", "muted"],
          [15, 17, "During", "Carbs if it runs past an hour", "accent"],
          [17, 19, "After", "Protein within about 2 h", "muted"],
        ];
        win.forEach(([a, b, k, t, c]) => {
          s.label(k, { x: X(a) + 0.15, y: top + 0.18, w: X(b) - X(a) - 0.3, color: c, size: 15 });
          s.text(X(a) + 0.15, top + 0.6, X(b) - X(a) - 0.3, 1.3, t, { font: "light", size: 20, lineH: 1.15 });
        });
        s.text(X(19) + 0.2, base - 0.85, 2.0, 0.75, "Illustrative day, times are examples", { font: "light", size: 13, color: "muted", lineH: 1.1 });
        s.source("Thomas, Erdman & Burke 2016 · Kerksick et al. 2017", { y: 6.2, refs: ["thomas2016", "kerksick2017"] });
        s.cite("kerksick2008");
      },
    },
    {
      role: "application", time: 60,
      notes: `Here is how that plays out on our calendar. Two situations come up every week.

When the session is early and there is no time for a meal, the answer is not to skip it. Something light and easy on the stomach on the way in, then a real breakfast after. The position stand's advice is to keep high-fat and high-fiber foods away from right before training.

When there are two sessions less than eight hours apart, refuel right after the first: carbohydrate with some protein, not just water and a wait.

The exact amounts belong to the sports dietitian, because they depend on the athlete. Our job is to make sure the schedule leaves room to eat.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("Match the food to the schedule.", { y: 1.2, w: 11.5, h: 0.9, size: 44 });
        const c1 = 0.75, c2 = 6.2;
        s.label("When", { x: c1, y: 2.55, w: 3 });
        s.label("Do", { x: c2, y: 2.55, w: 3 });
        s.line(0.75, 2.98, 12.58, 2.98, { color: "rule", w: 1 });
        const rows = [
          ["Early session, no time for a meal", "Something light before, a real breakfast after"],
          ["Two sessions less than 8 h apart", "Carbs and protein right after the first"],
        ];
        rows.forEach(([w, d], i) => {
          const y = 3.2 + i * 1.5;
          s.text(c1, y, 5.0, 1.1, w, { font: "light", size: 26, lineH: 1.12 });
          s.text(c2, y, 6.3, 1.1, d, { font: "medium", size: 26, lineH: 1.12 });
          s.line(0.75, y + 1.3, 12.58, y + 1.3, { color: "rule", w: 1 });
        });
        s.source("Thomas, Erdman & Burke 2016", { y: 6.2, refs: ["thomas2016"] });
      },
    },
    {
      role: "takeaway", time: 45,
      notes: `The line to take away: feed the day, then time it. Most of the benefit comes from athletes eating enough, regularly. Timing is the refinement on top, and it matters most on long days and short turnarounds.

Question for the room: where in our schedule do athletes train on empty? Early lifts, class running into practice, the bus leaving before the meal is ready. The answers tend to be specific, and most of them are scheduling problems we can actually fix.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("Feed the day. Then time it.", { y: 1.3, w: 7.5, h: 2.2, size: 72 });
        s.text(6.1, 4.25, 6.48, 1.9, "Where in our schedule do athletes train on empty?", { font: "medium", size: 34, color: "accent", lineH: 1.12, align: "right" });
      },
    },
  ],
};

