// 22 · Hydration Beyond Water: sweat carries salt, and how much varies a lot.
// Composition: hook = two plain columns, what we assume vs what gets measured; takeaway =
// big line, short rule, question; alignment = left. Insight is headline-dominant.

module.exports = {
  num: 22,
  slug: "hydration-beyond-water",
  title: "Hydration Beyond Water",
  category: "Nutrition & Fueling",
  palette: "baylor",
  hero: { layout: "range-bar-single-figure", concept: "One sweat-sodium bar from 10 to 70 mmol per liter (ACSM), a dashed extension to 100 (NATA), and a single marked average of about 35" },
  compose: { hook: "assume-vs-measured", takeaway: "headline-rule-question", align: "left" },
  oneIdea: "Sweat is water and salt, and athletes differ a lot in both; long, hot sessions and salty sweaters need more than plain water.",
  question: "Which of our teams has never had its sweat losses checked?",
  caveats: `Present the 10-70 mmol/L range and the ~35 average as the ACSM position stand's figures (Sawka et al. 2007); the NATA statement gives 10-100 mEq/L (McDermott et al. 2017). The two are the same unit for sodium. Don't give anyone a personal sodium target from this slide; that is the sports dietitian's and Athletic Medicine's call. The by-sport sweat rates on slide 1 are group averages with wide spread (football 1.51 ± 0.70 L/h; baseball 0.83 ± 0.34 L/h) from Barnes et al. 2019, whose authors work for the Gatorade Sports Science Institute, a sports-drink maker's research arm; Baker 2017 (same institute) explains why sweat-sodium tests vary with method. Over-drinking is a real risk too: drinking well beyond sweat losses can cause hyponatremia (Thomas et al. 2016). Weighing before and after practice is for fluid replacement only: done privately by Athletic Medicine or the sports dietitian, never posted, never tied to weight goals. This matches deck 25 (Body Composition Conversations).`,
  sources: [
    { id: "sawka2007", cite: "American College of Sports Medicine; Sawka MN, Burke LM, Eichner ER, Maughan RJ, Montain SJ, Stachenfeld NS. American College of Sports Medicine position stand. Exercise and fluid replacement. Med Sci Sports Exerc. 2007;39(2):377-390. doi:10.1249/mss.0b013e31802ca597", grade: "position stand", found: "external (cited in Zotero item Desbrow et al. 2015)", url: "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/17277604/", supports: "Sweat sodium averages ~35 mEq/L (range 10-70); drink to prevent >2% body weight loss to avert compromised performance; sweat rates and electrolyte content vary between individuals, so plans should be customized; sweat rate can be estimated from body weight before and after exercise." },
    { id: "mcdermott2017", cite: "McDermott BP, Anderson SA, Armstrong LE, et al. National Athletic Trainers' Association position statement: fluid replacement for the physically active. J Athl Train. 2017;52(9):877-895. doi:10.4085/1062-6050-52.9.02", grade: "position statement", found: "external", url: "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28985128/", supports: "'Sweat sodium concentrations, for example, range from 10 to 100 mEq/L, and these large variations require individual replacement strategies.'" },
    { id: "barnes2019", cite: "Barnes KA, Anderson ML, Stofan JR, et al. Normative data for sweating rate, sweat sodium concentration, and sweat sodium loss in athletes: an update and analysis by sport. J Sports Sci. 2019;37(20):2356-2366. doi:10.1080/02640414.2019.1633159", grade: "large observational dataset (n = 1303; industry-funded lab)", found: "external", url: "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31230518/", supports: "Whole-body sweating rate averaged 1.51 ± 0.70 L/h in American football and 0.83 ± 0.34 L/h in baseball." },
    { id: "baker2017", cite: "Baker LB. Sweating rate and sweat sodium concentration in athletes: a review of methodology and intra/interindividual variability. Sports Med. 2017;47(Suppl 1):111-128. doi:10.1007/s40279-017-0691-5", grade: "narrative review (industry-funded lab)", found: "external", url: "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28332116/", supports: "Sweat rate and composition vary considerably within and between individuals; results depend heavily on test method." },
    { id: "thomas2016", cite: "Thomas DT, Erdman KA, Burke LM. American College of Sports Medicine joint position statement. Nutrition and athletic performance. Med Sci Sports Exerc. 2016;48(3):543-568. doi:10.1249/MSS.0000000000000852", grade: "joint position stand", found: "external", url: "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26891166/", supports: "Sweat contains substantial but variable sodium; over-drinking beyond sweat and urine losses is the primary cause of hyponatremia." },
    { id: "desbrow2015", cite: "Desbrow B, Cecchin D, Jones A, Grant G, Irwin C, Leveritt M. Manipulations to the alcohol and sodium content of beer for postexercise rehydration. Int J Sport Nutr Exerc Metab. 2015;25(3):262-270. doi:10.1123/ijsnem.2014-0064", grade: "small crossover trial (n = 12)", found: "Zotero", supports: "Rehydration should replace both water and sodium; sodium in the recovery period helps fluid retention (p. 5). More sodium in the drink meant less urine lost." },
    { id: "fullagar2017", cite: "Fullagar HHK, McCunn R, Murray A. Updated review of the applied physiology of American college football: physical demands, strength and conditioning, nutrition, and injury characteristics of America's favorite game. Int J Sports Physiol Perform. 2017;12(10):1396-1403. doi:10.1123/ijspp.2016-0783", grade: "narrative review", found: "Zotero", supports: "College football players appear to have limited nutrition and hydration practices, which may hurt performance." },
  ],
  slides: [
    {
      role: "hook", time: 45,
      notes: `Most of us run hydration on one assumption: everyone needs about the same, so put out water and remind people to drink.

Here is what happens when people actually measure. In a large testing database of over thirteen hundred athletes, football players averaged about one and a half liters of sweat an hour. Baseball players averaged a bit over half that. And those are averages; individuals inside each sport spread out a long way.

So one water cooler for everyone is a guess. Today is about what else is in the bottle.`,
      build(s) {
        s.label("What we assume", { x: 0.75, y: 1.6, w: 5 });
        s.text(0.75, 2.1, 5.2, 2.6, "Everyone needs about the same water.", { font: "condBold", size: 44, lineH: 1.05 });
        s.line(6.62, 1.65, 6.62, 5.6, { color: "rule", w: 1 });
        s.label("What gets measured", { x: 7.1, y: 1.6, w: 5 });
        s.text(7.1, 2.1, 5.48, 3.2, ["Football players averaged ", { t: "about 1.5 liters", color: "accent" }, " of sweat an hour. Baseball players, about 0.8."], { font: "light", size: 30, lineH: 1.18 });
        s.source("Barnes et al. 2019, 1,303 athletes tested", { x: 7.1, y: 5.95, w: 5.4, refs: ["barnes2019"] });
        s.cite("fullagar2017");
      },
    },
    {
      role: "problem", time: 60,
      notes: `Here is the problem with water-only thinking. Sweat is not just water. It carries sodium, and that matters three ways.

First, falling behind costs performance. The ACSM's line is to avoid losing more than about two percent of body weight to sweat during exercise.

Second, more water is not always safer. Drinking far beyond what you sweat can dilute the sodium in the blood, which is dangerous. That is the main cause of hyponatremia in sport.

Third, after a session, sodium helps the body hold on to what you drink. Without it, more of the fluid goes straight through.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("Sweat takes salt too.", { y: 1.2, w: 11.5, h: 0.9, size: 48 });
        const rows = [
          "Losing over 2% of body weight to sweat can hurt performance.",
          "Drinking far more than you sweat can dilute blood sodium.",
          "Sodium after exercise helps the body hold on to fluid.",
        ];
        rows.forEach((t, i) => {
          const y = 2.55 + i * 1.15;
          s.line(0.75, y, 12.58, y, { color: "rule", w: 1 });
          s.text(0.75, y + 0.22, 11.0, 0.8, t, { font: "light", size: 28, lineH: 1.1 });
        });
        s.line(0.75, 6.0, 12.58, 6.0, { color: "rule", w: 1 });
        s.source("Sawka et al. 2007 (ACSM) · Thomas, Erdman & Burke 2016 · Desbrow et al. 2015", { y: 6.15, w: 10, refs: ["sawka2007", "thomas2016", "desbrow2015"] });
      },
    },
    {
      role: "insight", time: 75,
      notes: `Here is the insight. How salty sweat is varies a lot from one athlete to the next.

The bar is sweat sodium, in millimoles per liter. The ACSM position stand puts the usual range at about ten to seventy, with an average around thirty-five. The athletic trainers' statement puts the top end nearer a hundred. Either way, two athletes in the same drill can lose several times as much salt as each other.

You can sometimes spot the salty sweaters: white streaks on dark gear, a salty taste. But the honest point is that sweat testing is noisy, and the method matters, so this is one for Athletic Medicine and the sports dietitian to measure properly.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("Some athletes lose several times more salt in sweat than others.", { y: 1.2, w: 8.6, h: 2.0, size: 56 });
        const X = v => 1.0 + v * 0.105; // 0 to 100 mmol/L
        const y = 5.0;
        s.line(X(0), y, X(100), y, { color: "rule", w: 1 });
        s.line(X(10), y, X(70), y, { color: "muted", w: 14 });
        s.line(X(70), y, X(100), y, { color: "muted", w: 2, dash: "dash" });
        s.line(X(35), y - 0.42, X(35), y + 0.42, { color: "accent", w: 4 });
        s.label("Average, about 35", { x: X(35) - 1.5, y: y - 0.9, w: 3.0, align: "center", color: "accent", size: 16 });
        s.label("10", { x: X(10) - 0.5, y: y + 0.3, w: 1.0, align: "center", size: 16 });
        s.label("70", { x: X(70) - 0.5, y: y + 0.3, w: 1.0, align: "center", size: 16 });
        s.label("Up to 100 in some", { x: X(100) - 2.5, y: y + 0.3, w: 2.5, align: "right", size: 14 });
        s.label("Sodium in sweat, mmol per liter", { x: X(0), y: y + 0.3, w: 0.01 + 0.0, size: 14 });
        s.source("10–70 and ~35: Sawka et al. 2007 (ACSM) · up to 100: McDermott et al. 2017 (NATA)", { y: 6.2, w: 10, refs: ["sawka2007", "mcdermott2017"] });
        s.cite("baker2017");
      },
    },
    {
      role: "application", time: 60,
      notes: `So what can we do on Monday? Weigh before and after a hot practice. The drop over a session is mostly sweat, and it tells you how much fluid that athlete needs to put back. For the heavy, salty sweaters, the plan includes sodium, not just water.

One thing matters a lot here. This is a fluid number, nothing else. It is taken privately by Athletic Medicine or the sports dietitian, it is never posted on a wall, and it is never tied to weight goals. Our next deck in this series, on body composition, explains why that line matters.`,
      build(s) {
        s.rect(9.0, 0, 4.333, 7.5, { fill: "soft" });
        s.headline("Weigh before and after hot practices, in private.", { y: 1.6, w: 7.6, h: 2.0, size: 52 });
        s.lead("The drop is mostly sweat. Replace it with fluid, and with sodium for salty sweaters.", { y: 3.95, w: 7.2, h: 1.4, size: 24 });
        s.label("Who owns it", { x: 9.5, y: 1.75, w: 3.2 });
        s.text(9.5, 2.2, 3.1, 2.6, "Athletic Medicine and the sports dietitian. A fluid number only, never posted.", { font: "light", size: 22, lineH: 1.22 });
        s.source("Sawka et al. 2007 (ACSM) · McDermott et al. 2017 (NATA)", { y: 6.2, w: 7.6, refs: ["sawka2007", "mcdermott2017"] });
      },
    },
    {
      role: "takeaway", time: 45,
      notes: `The line to take away: put back what sweat takes. For most sessions that is water and normal meals. For long, hot sessions and salty sweaters, it means sodium as well, planned by the people who measure it.

Question for the room: which of our teams has never had its sweat losses checked? August practices are the obvious place to start.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("Put back what sweat takes.", { y: 1.35, w: 11.5, h: 1.5, size: 88 });
        s.lead("Long, hot sessions and salty sweaters need sodium as well as water.", { y: 3.2, w: 9.2, h: 1.0, size: 24 });
        s.line(0.75, 4.75, 1.75, 4.75, { color: "accent", w: 3 });
        s.text(0.75, 4.95, 11.8, 1.0, "Which of our teams has never had its sweat losses checked?", { font: "medium", size: 30, color: "accent", lineH: 1.15 });
      },
    },
  ],
};

